<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Liminal — macOS hardened-runtime entitlements.

  Used during electron-builder's signing step on macOS. Hardened runtime
  is required for notarization, and notarization is required for an
  app distributed outside the Mac App Store to launch cleanly on
  Sequoia and later without Gatekeeper warnings.

  Hardened runtime disables a bunch of legacy capabilities by default
  (JIT, library loading from non-canonical locations, etc.) — we have
  to opt back in for the ones Electron + Node + our helper binaries
  need. These do NOT loosen security for an end user; they relax the
  process's own self-restrictions so Liminal can function.

  Why each entitlement:

  - allow-jit: Electron's V8 engine generates and executes machine
    code at runtime for JavaScript. Without this, the renderer crashes
    on first JS execution under hardened runtime.

  - allow-unsigned-executable-memory: Electron's Node integration
    and some native node_modules ship modules that mmap executable
    memory not signed by Apple. Required for Electron to even boot.

  - allow-dyld-environment-variables: The Electron main process
    spawns the backend Node child via ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1, which
    is propagated through DYLD_*-style env variables. Hardened
    runtime would strip these on exec without this entitlement.

  - disable-library-validation: We load:
      * Node native modules (better-sqlite3, bcrypt, etc.)
      * Bundled PyInstaller binaries (tts_server, yubikey_helper)
      * Python's libraries inside those bundles
    Library validation would reject anything not signed by the same
    Team ID as the host app or by Apple. Our helpers are signed by us
    (correct Team ID), but their child .so/.dylib loads are noisy
    enough that the safer move is to disable this check.

  - device.audio-input: Required so the Speech-to-Text dictation
    feature can access the microphone. Paired with the
    NSMicrophoneUsageDescription string in Info.plist (set via
    package.json build.mac.extendInfo) so macOS shows a meaningful
    consent prompt the first time the user taps Dictate.

  Notably NOT here: app sandbox, network entitlements. We're using
  Developer ID distribution, not App Store, so we don't sandbox.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
